{"qid":"Q207521","names":["Ethiopian Empire","Armenia"],"title":"Ethiopian Empire","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Ethiopian Empire (Solomonic dynasty) was a Christian highland monarchy that claimed descent from Solomon and the Queen of Sheba, restored by Yekuno Amlak in 1270 after defeating the Zagwe dynasty. At its apogee under Emperor Zara Yaqob (r. 1434–1468) and again under Menelik II (r. 1889–1913), who defeated the invading Italian army at Adwa on 1 March 1896 and preserved Ethiopian independence through the colonial era, the empire controlled the entire Horn of Africa highland region and portions of the Nile drainage. Italian forces invaded and occupied Ethiopia 1935–1936 under Mussolini, with Emperor Haile Selassie restored by British forces in 1941; the empire continued until 1974 when a Marxist military junta (the Derg) deposed and ultimately killed Haile Selassie, though the dataset attests the polity through 1935.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Ethiopian Empire's core highland territory is moderately attested through royal chronicles (tarika nagast), land grants (stelae and parchment documents), and Portuguese and Ottoman diplomatic records; peripheral lowland and tributary regions are approximated and carry significant uncertainty, especially before the 19th century Survey expeditions.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Ethiopia","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Ethiopia"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Ethiopian Empire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethiopian_Empire"}],"britannica":"place/Abyssinia","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Ethiopian Empire","rulers":[{"qid":"Q8880